Z||000000||Lisa 86 2m4||03-12-2001||03:02 PM||||can a girl and a dremmel......<BR>ok, I'm on my way to pick up a new damper, and I'm really thinking about adding a rear sway bar, but here's the question, how easy is it? I found a site that for $27 you can buy a front bar (www.gmpartsusa.com), but that sounds really tricky to do. JC Whitney says they have a custom rear for $99. Should I just go that route?How difficult is it to install? Any special tools required?  I want easy and the most effective, if that means $50 more for the rear, I"d rather do that.<BR>How difficult is it to replace the front bushings and endlink? <BR>Thanks! I'm so glad I have you to look to for advice!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Will||03-12-2001||04:58 PM||||I would recommend going to poly rear control arm bushings and poly or aluminum cradle bushings before or at the same time you put the rear swaybar on.<P>You will need to drill holes in the bottom of the cradle. While it is not necessary, I recommend drilling the pre-existing holes in the control arms out to the step diameter of the end link bushing you will use.<BR>The way I did it was to cut two strips of steel, about 6" long, 1" wide, and 5/16" thick or so, and drill 4 holes in each: 2 clearance holes for 3/8" bolts, about 5" apart on center, and 2 tap holes for 5/16" bolts which would take the sway bar bracket bolts. Where I wanted to put it, the swaybar went right under the forward pivot of the control arm. I used the strips of steel to move the bolts in the bottom of the cradle further apart so that they cleared the sides of the control arm pivot. I then bolted the sway bar bracket to the steel strip which is bolted to the cradle.<P>Do you have a jack and jackstands? Once the front end is in the air, working on sway bar stuff is easy and requires no specialized tools.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 03-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||03-12-2001||06:36 PM||||Oh, you meant multi-questions, not multi-steering.  For a minute there I thought you wanted to be able to turn like a monster truck.  That toggle-switch rear steering would be a real trick to install on a Fiero, but imagine what it would be like!<P>||||reg||6
